        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "944f421e-37bf-506f-bae6-5f4cbf11136e",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Lincoln Steffens, Monique Wittig, Doc Ames\n- Works: McClure's, \"Tweed Days in St. Louis\", \"The Shame of the Cities\"\n- Places: New York, St. Louis, Minneapolis\n- Topics: journalism, feminism, gender roles, organized crime\n\nFacts:\n- Lincoln Joseph Steffens was a New York reporter born on April 6, 1866, and he launched a series of articles in McClure's called \"Tweed Days in St. Louis.\"\n- In 1903, organized crime in Minneapolis first attracted national attention when Doc Ames, a thug and mayor of Minneapolis, was exposed by Lincoln Steffens in the book \"The Shame of the Cities.\"\n- Lincoln Steffens's \"Tweed Days in St. Louis\" articles were later published together as a book titled \"The Shame of the Cities.\"\n- Monique Wittig was a French author and feminist theorist born on July 13, 1935, who wrote about overcoming socially enforced gender roles and coined the phrase \"heterosexual contract.\"\n- Lincoln Steffens died on August 9, 1936.\n- Monique Wittig died on January 3, 2003.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
